import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Demos/Lobby

meta = {
	title: "Live and channels",
	description: "Live features on the Roc Solid server platform: channels push typed, access-checked messages to pages over one stream per tab, with hooks for what pages say and who comes and goes; rooms and topics underneath.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "channels", label: "Channels: live data for pages" },
		{ id: "channel-hooks", label: "What pages say, and hooks" },
		{ id: "channel-stream", label: "One stream per tab" },
		{ id: "rooms", label: "Rooms: who is here" },
		{ id: "topics", label: "Topics: messages between calls" },
		{ id: "which", label: "Which to use" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · live and channels"
				title="Live updates, from the server to the page"
			>
				Some pages change while someone looks at them: a chat, a shared board, who is online. The server
				pushes messages to them, usually on a <strong>channel</strong>: a typed stream pages subscribe to,
				checked against who may read it. Channels are built on
				<strong>rooms</strong> and <strong>topics</strong>, which your own event streams can use directly.
				All of it lives in the server's memory; what must survive a restart belongs in an
				<a href={Paths.platform_actors}>actor</a> or a <a href={Paths.platform_database}>table</a>.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="channels">Channels: live data for pages</Heading>
			<p>
				A channel lives in a route's <code>+server.roc</code>, beside its endpoints, so it has a path. Its
				definition, <code>channel!</code>, is a check: given the request, the path's params and what the
				route's <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> handed on, it returns who the
				subscriber is (<code>Channel.allow(who)</code>) or a refusal. Its type says what the server
				publishes and what pages may say. This page's lobby lets everyone in:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.channel_lobby.file} source={Sources.channel_lobby.rocx} />
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo waiting="Join, here and in another tab.">
			<div data-demo="lobby"><Lobby /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> puts each channel in <code>Generated/Api</code> as a function of its params,
				like endpoints: <code>Api.channel_api_platform_live_lobby({"{}"})</code> here,
				<code>Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org }"})</code> for <code>/org/[org]/news</code>. Its type is the
				<code>Channel(who, message, said)</code> from <code>channel!</code>'s annotation.
				<code>Generated/Api</code> imports no route file, so a job, an actor or any server module can import
				it and publish on a channel by name, type-checked.
			</p>
			<p>
				A component subscribes from an <a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect</a>. The subscription
				ends when the effect runs again or the component goes away:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_lobby.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_lobby.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>A subscriber hears these events, in order, one at a time:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Resync</code></strong>: the page may have missed something, so it should read its
					data again (<code>refresh!</code>). Sent each time the tab's stream connects or reconnects, when
					the subscriber fell behind, and when a page asks (<code>channel.resync!()</code>). The route's
					<code>on_open!</code> messages follow it.
				</li>
				<li><strong><code>Message(message)</code></strong>: something published, as the channel's type.</li>
				<li><strong><code>Refused(status, why)</code></strong>: the check refused the subscriber when the stream opened.</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Ended(why)</code></strong>: the check ran again at a beat (every 15 s) and refused
					it. Someone who loses access stops hearing within a beat.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The server publishes with
				<code>Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org: \"acme\" }"}).publish!(news)</code>, from a request, a job
				or an actor. Only subscribers with the same params hear it, in publish order. A page's first data
				comes from a <code>get!</code> endpoint, often in the channel's file (<code>.read!()</code>); the
				channel carries the changes after that.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Only the latest matters?</strong> <code>channel.publish_latest!(key, message)</code>
					is for values where only the newest counts (a cursor's position). A subscriber that is behind
					gets only the newest message per key, unordered with the channel's other messages.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Falling behind.</strong> A subscriber more than 256 messages behind hears
					<code>Resync</code> instead of what it missed, never a silent gap.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="channel-hooks">What pages say, and hooks</Heading>
			<p>
				Beside <code>channel!</code>, a route may define three hooks. Each gets the path's params, who the
				subscriber is (from the check), its tab id, and the channel (to publish on):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>receive!</code></strong> handles what a page says
					(<code>channel.say!(said)</code>) after the same middleware and check, or refuses it.
					What a page says within one frame goes in one request.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_open!</code></strong> returns the messages a subscriber gets first, each time it
					subscribes (who is here, say), right after its <code>Resync</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_close!</code></strong> is told when a subscription ends, and why:
					<code>Dropped</code> (the tab stays but the page stopped subscribing: the component went, or
					follows another channel), <code>Gone</code> (the tab closed or reloaded, its network went, or the
					server stopped), or <code>Refused(why)</code> (the check, at a beat). It runs outside the
					middleware, since losing access may be what ended it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>who</code> stays on the server, as JSON, from the check until <code>on_close!</code>. A tab id
				is random and lasts the tab's life (a reload keeps it); it names a tab, not a person.
			</p>
			<p>
				A tab's hooks run in order. If a page drops a channel and subscribes again, the route hears the end
				first. If a tab's new stream (after a reload or reconnect) arrives before the server noticed the old
				one end, the old one ends first: its <code>on_close!</code> calls run before the new stream's checks
				and <code>on_open!</code>. So a route can keep "who is here" per tab with nothing to count by hand:
				join on <code>on_open!</code>, leave on <code>on_close!</code> (at once when <code>Dropped</code>;
				after a moment when <code>Gone</code>, in case the tab comes back). The
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_presence}>presence package</a> does this for you, with names, colours and
				cursors.
			</p>

			<Heading id="channel-stream">One stream per tab</Heading>
			<p>
				All of a tab's subscriptions share one server-sent events stream,
				<code>GET /rocx/live?tab=…&amp;c=/org/acme/news&amp;c=…</code>, and two components on one channel
				share one subscription. The browser's runtime opens the stream once the page has rendered. If it
				drops, the runtime reopens it after a wait that doubles each time, from half a second up to 30 s.
			</p>
			<p>
				When the page's subscriptions change (after the render that changed them), the runtime posts the
				change, naming the stream's id, and the stream stays open. With nothing left to
				subscribe to, the runtime says so, then closes the stream. A static copy of a site (files, no
				server) has no channels: a subscriber hears <code>Refused(404, …)</code>, once.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>On the stream</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>event: hello</code>, <code>data: </code> the stream's id</td><td>first: the id the runtime uses to change the tab's subscriptions (192 random bits, unique to this stream)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: resync</code>, <code>data: /org/acme/news</code></td><td>read your data again; <code>on_open!</code>'s messages follow</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: message</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"title\":…}"}</code></td><td>what was published, as JSON</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: refused</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"status\":403,\"why\":\"Forbidden\"}"}</code></td><td>the check refused this channel; the stream goes on for the others</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: ended</code>, the URL, then why, as a JSON string</td><td>the check refused it at a beat</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>: beat</code></td><td>every 15 s (<code>--live-beat-ms</code>): the server is there, and has checked each channel again</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: replaced</code></td><td>a newer stream for this tab replaced this one, which ends. A page still reading it is a second tab with the same id (a duplicated tab copies it); it takes its own id</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>No call is held open.</strong> The host keeps the open streams and passes messages on. Roc
					runs only briefly: a check, a hook, a beat's checks.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Middleware applies.</strong> Each check is a request to the app for the channel's path,
					with the tab's cookies, behind the app's and the route's middleware, like a <code>GET</code> of
					that path. The cookies are the stream's, or the last subscription change's, so a page that signs
					in without a reload reaches members' channels. A sign-in guard guards a channel by its path;
					<code>/rocx/live</code> itself never reaches the app's middleware.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A lost tab is gone in about 35 s.</strong> A tab that lost its network never closes its
					stream. The next beat goes unacknowledged; 20 s later the server drops the connection and tells
					<code>on_close!</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What pages say</strong> goes in <code>POST /rocx/live/send?tab=…&amp;stream=…</code>: a JSON
					list of <code>{"{\"channel\":\"/org/acme/news\",\"message\":…}"}</code>, with the stream's changes
					(<code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"subscribe\":true}"}</code>, <code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"unsubscribe\":true}"}</code>).
					The answer is 204, or 200 with each refusal
					(<code>{"{\"refused\":[{\"index\":0,\"status\":403,\"why\":…}]}"}</code>). A post from another
					site's page is refused (403), and so is a change naming no open stream of the tab (404).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Limits:</strong> a stream carries at most 64 channels; a post carries at most 256 items
					and 1 MiB.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="rooms">Rooms: who is here</Heading>
			<p>
				Rooms and topics are the building blocks under channels, for event streams you write yourself
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#streams"}>streams</a>). A room counts who is in it. A call joins one
				with <code>Live.join!</code> and stays until the call ends: for a stream, until its client goes or it
				crashes. <code>Live.count!</code> reads a room's count from anywhere. This stream sends the count at
				the start and whenever someone comes or goes:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_online.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_online.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Live.next!(ms)</code> waits for what the call hears: <code>Heard</code> (a message on a topic
				it listens to, or someone joining or leaving its room), <code>Quiet</code> after <code>ms</code>
				milliseconds of nothing, or <code>Gone</code> when the client has gone. So this loop sleeps until
				there is something to say, and ends with its client.
			</p>

			<Heading id="topics">Topics: messages between calls</Heading>
			<p>
				A topic carries messages to every call listening to it. <code>Live.listen!</code> listens for the
				rest of the call. <code>Live.publish!</code>, from any request, job or actor, sends to every
				listener:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_shouts.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_shouts.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A component listens to such a stream like any endpoint's <a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a>.
				On the server, it is a call that stays open: it holds no thread while it waits, but keeps its stack.
				In the browser, it takes one of the six connections a browser opens to a host over HTTP/1.1. And
				there is one per page, not one per tab. For what pages follow live, a channel is better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="which">Which to use</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>For</th><th>Use</th><th>Lasts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>live data for pages, checked</td><td>a channel (<code>channel!</code>, <code>.subscribe!</code>, <code>.publish!</code>)</td><td>while the subscribers last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>who is here, now</td><td>a room (<code>Live.join!</code>, <code>Live.count!</code>), or a channel's hooks</td><td>while the calls in it last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>telling every listening call</td><td>a topic (<code>Live.publish!</code>, <code>Live.listen!</code>)</td><td>while the listeners last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a count, a cache, a token used once</td><td>a value in memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}><code>Kv</code></a>: <code>Kv.increment!</code>, <code>Kv.set!</code>, <code>Kv.take!</code>)</td><td>its time to live, or until a restart</td></tr>
						<tr><td>state many requests change, one at a time</td><td>an actor (<a href={Paths.platform_actors}><code>Actor</code></a>: <code>ask!</code>, <code>tell!</code>)</td><td>in the database: survives restarts</td></tr>
						<tr><td>data, queried every way</td><td>a table (<a href={Paths.platform_database}><code>Db</code></a>)</td><td>in the database</td></tr>
						<tr><td>work that may fail and should run again</td><td>a job (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}><code>Job.enqueue!</code></a>)</td><td>in the database, until done</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests cover rooms, topics, many channels on one stream (one refused), hooks, a
					check failing at a beat, falling behind, and refused posts. 40 streams stay open on two Roc threads while requests still
					answer. In a browser, two components share one stream, a change needs no reconnect, and a dropped
					stream reconnects a little later each time.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
